WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.620
The first film separated a variant's origin from selection.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.280
Now a DNA strip is damaged; is the cell mutated yet?

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.040
Follow how that variant begins.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.600
The red mark appears before any daughter sequence has formed.

5
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:22.963
A mutation is a heritable change in DNA sequence.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.543
Heritable matters here.

7
00:00:26.563 --> 00:00:31.283
A temporary chemical lesion is damage, but it is not automatically a changed

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:35.983
sequence. The sequence must change and persist when the cell divides.

9
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:39.577
Changes can begin in two broad ways.

10
00:00:40.977 --> 00:00:42.417
Copying can place the wrong block.

11
00:00:43.557 --> 00:00:46.817
Or chemistry can damage a block before the next copy.

12
00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:50.177
Either route can end without a mutation.

13
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.920
Damage enters a fork.

14
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.080
Repair may restore the original block.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.300
If repair misses it, copying may preserve the original sequence or produce a

16
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.480
different one. The lesion's presence does not choose the outcome.

17
00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:12.603
Before the strip reaches that fork, predict: must a red damage mark become a

18
00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:16.743
mutation? Trace every possible path before choosing.

19
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.720
No. On one path, repair removes the lesion and restores the blue block.

20
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.480
After copying, both daughter strips keep the original sequence.

21
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.520
The cell had damage, yet no mutation remains.

22
00:01:38.683 --> 00:01:42.843
On another path, copying places a wrong block opposite the lesion.

23
00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:47.863
At the next copy, that wrong block templates a full base-pair change.

24
00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:53.823
The damaged strand goes to a separate daughter, where repair or bypass can follow.

25
00:01:55.103 --> 00:01:59.043
In the changed lineage the lesion is gone, but its consequence persists.

26
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:06.197
Internal chemistry and ordinary copying create spontaneous mutations without an

27
00:02:06.197 --> 00:02:11.677
applied mutagen. Outside agents are mutagens when they raise mutation.

28
00:02:12.977 --> 00:02:14.417
Both routes end the same way.

29
00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:21.017
A change becomes a mutation only if repair or copying leaves a heritable sequence

30
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:22.017
change.

31
00:02:22.617 --> 00:02:28.057
The effect on a protein may range from none to loss of function, and occasionally a

32
00:02:28.057 --> 00:02:29.657
changed or gained function.

33
00:02:30.937 --> 00:02:34.317
Those consequences come after the inherited sequence change.

34
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.760
Only after a heritable variant exists can an environment change its representation.

35
00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.220
The first film followed that later sorting step with phage resistance.

36
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.160
Selection cannot reach backward and request that earlier change.

37
00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:57.773
Damage becomes mutation when repair or copying leaves a heritable sequence change.

38
00:02:59.373 --> 00:03:00.853
Repair can erase the damage first.

39
00:03:02.093 --> 00:03:05.133
The inherited sequence, not the lesion, is the mutation.

40
00:03:06.453 --> 00:03:09.653
A red mark alone never satisfies the definition.

41
00:03:10.817 --> 00:03:14.457
The lesson now names the specific sources of these changes.

42
00:03:15.777 --> 00:03:20.277
Then the Ames test asks whether a chemical raises mutations in bacteria.

43
00:03:21.697 --> 00:03:24.057
Those details belong in the text that follows.
